    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0149" n="129" xml:id="pg129"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795B.5/6–2550: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Korea</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Muccio</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">niact</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-06-25T20:00:00+10:00">June 25, 1950—8 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 25—2:05 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>929. KMAG has just been impelled to despatch urgent telegram to CINCFE, repeated
                    DeptAr (WRI 077) appealing to CINCFE to ship ten day supply of certain items of
                    ammunition at once to Pusan for Korean Army.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d65-f1"/> KMAG
                    found it necessary to make this urgent appeal because ammunition is critically
                    needed to meet situation arising from widespread Communist attacks today along
                    parallel. For an account of Korean ammunition situation, Department is referred
                    to semi-annual KMAG report which was air pouched yesterday under cover Embdes
                        661.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d65-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>I earnestly appeal to Department to back up to such extent as may be necessary
                    KMAG’s appeal for additional ammunition. Without early receipt such ammunition
                    and assuming hostilities continue at present level, is feared modest stocks in
                    Korean hands will be exhausted within ten days time. In my opinion, it would be
                    catastrophic for US to permit gallant Korean forces to succumb for lack of
                    ammunition. I am confident that if adequately supplied, ROK security forces will
                    fight bravely and with distinction.</p>
                <p>Repeated information CINCFE.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Muccio</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Note:</hi> Mr. Connors (FE) informed 2:26 a. m.,
                        6/25/50 (CWO) FMH.</p>
                    <p>Relayed to Tokyo, 4 a. m., 6/25/FMH.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d65-f1" n="1" xml:id="d65-f1-content">
                <p>The request called for ammunition to support 90 105-mm. howitzers, 700 60-mm.
                    mortars, and 40,000 .30-caliber carbines; see Sawyer, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Military Advisors in Korea</hi>, <ref target="#pg120">p. 120</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d65-f2" n="2" xml:id="d65-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed, but see footnote 3 to despatch no. 660, June 23, from Seoul, <ref
                        target="#pg122">p. 122</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
